window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][3]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M3-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M3-L1 – Conductivity, Charge Carriers and Semiconductors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Semiconductors, Analogue Electronics and Digital Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Current and\ncharge<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Resistance\nand resistivity<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain the difference between\nelectrons and holes, classify dopants as donors or acceptors, and\ncalculate conductivity from carrier densities and mobilities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why silicon can fulfil both roles<\/h2>\n<p>A silicon sample can have a high resistance or a substantially lower\nresistance for the same geometry. The decisive factor is the number of\nmobile charge carriers available. Introducing impurity atoms\ndeliberately allows this number to be changed. Different parts of a\ncrystal can thus acquire different electrical properties. The pn\njunction introduced later exploits precisely this possibility.<\/p>\n<p>At low temperatures, the <strong>valence band<\/strong> contains most\nof the electrons involved in bonding within the crystal. In the\n<strong>conduction band<\/strong>, electrons can contribute to electrical\nconduction. Between the two lies the <strong>band gap<\/strong>, an\nenergy range with no allowed states of the undisturbed crystal. The\nfigure arranges states by energy. Its horizontal direction does not\nrepresent a spatial coordinate here.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_010_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Energy bands of pure, n-doped and p-doped silicon. Donor states lie below the conduction band, acceptor states above the valence band.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: In the simplified silicon band model, donor levels\nsit just below the conduction band and acceptor levels just above the\nvalence band.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>In a pure semiconductor, thermal excitation can promote an electron\nfrom the valence band to the conduction band. A vacancy remains in the\nvalence band. Adjacent electrons can occupy this vacancy, leaving a new\nvacancy elsewhere each time. For transport purposes, the vacancy can be\ndescribed as a <strong>hole<\/strong> with positive charge. When\nelectron-hole pairs are generated, the densities of both types of\nparticle are equal in a pure semiconductor.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Donors and acceptors<\/h2>\n<p>With sufficient thermal excitation, a donor in silicon supplies an\nelectron to the conduction band. The ionised donor atom remains\nstationary and positively charged. Electrons are the majority carriers\nin this <strong>n-doped<\/strong> region. An acceptor can capture an\nelectron from the valence band. It remains bound to the stationary\nacceptor as a negative charge, leaving a mobile hole behind. In a\n<strong>p-doped<\/strong> region, holes are the majority carriers.<\/p>\n<p>An n-doped region therefore generally has no net negative charge in\nits bulk. Both mobile electrons and stationary ionised dopant atoms\ncontribute to the charge balance. Thermal generation also produces\nminority carriers in doped samples. Their number can be crucial for\ndevices, although they often contribute little to the conductivity of a\nuniformly heavily doped sample.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Electron and hole currents in a numerical model<\/h2>\n<p>For a homogeneous material in a weak field, we use positive\nmobilities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor electrons and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor holes. By convention, both current contributions are in the\ndirection of the field. For electrons, the negative charge and the\nopposite drift direction both reverse the sign. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.602<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=1.602\\cdot 10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nelectron density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand hole density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe conductivity is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=q(n\\mu_n+p\\mu_p).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With densities in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand mobilities in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m^2/(V\\,s)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nyou obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{S/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe resistivity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1/\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℓ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand cross-sectional area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\ell/(\\sigma A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For a simplified comparison model, take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.135<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_n=0.135\\,\\mathrm{m^2/(V\\,s)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.048<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_p=0.048\\,\\mathrm{m^2/(V\\,s)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet the intrinsic sample have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>16<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=p=10^{16}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.93<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma\\approx 2.93\\cdot 10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{S/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an n-doped sample with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>21<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=10^{21}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a negligible hole contribution, we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>21.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma\\approx21.6\\,\\mathrm{S/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA p-doped sample with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>21<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=10^{21}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a negligible electron contribution reaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.69<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7.69\\,\\mathrm{S/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEqual numbers of majority carriers can therefore conduct differently\nbecause their mobilities differ.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the resistance of the n-doped sample for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=1\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.01\\,\\mathrm{mm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat current does the model predict at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A p-doped sample contains twice as many mobile holes as in the\nexample. What follows if the mobility remains constant? Which part of\nthis assumption can fail at higher doping levels?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<p><strong>1.<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=10^{-8}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstitution gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.62<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\approx4.62\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand, using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=U/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>21.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">21.6\\,\\mathrm{\\mu A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>2.<\/strong> With a negligible electron contribution,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles to approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15.4\\,\\mathrm{S/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a real sample, mobility depends, among other factors, on scattering\nby impurity atoms and on temperature. Doubling the density alone then\ndoes not necessarily double the conductivity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Where the model ends<\/h2>\n<p>We treat densities and mobilities as prescribed, spatially uniform\nquantities. Contact resistance, diffusion, recombination and high fields\nare excluded from the comparison. When p and n regions meet, charge\ncarriers can redistribute even without an external voltage. This is\nexamined in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">the lesson on\nthe pn junction and rectification<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/9-6-semiconductors-and-doping\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 3, § 9.6 “Semiconductors and Doping”<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 9.6 – conduction band, holes and doping. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-012-microelectronic-devices-and-circuits-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.012 “Microelectronic Devices and Circuits”, lecture\nnotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · charge carriers, drift and semiconductor fundamentals. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M3-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M3-L2 – PN Junction, Diode and Rectification<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Semiconductors, Analogue Electronics and Digital Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Charge\ncarriers and doping<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Ohm’s law<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Kirchhoff’s\nvoltage law<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify reverse and forward\nbias, select a suitable diode model and calculate the peak and mean\nvalues of half-wave rectification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What happens at the interface<\/h2>\n<p>When a p region and an n region come into contact, electrons and\nholes initially diffuse across the junction because of their\nconcentration differences. Some of them recombine near the junction.\nStationary positively ionised donors remain on the n side and negatively\nionised acceptors on the p side. The resulting <strong>space-charge\nregion<\/strong> (depletion region) contains relatively few mobile\ncarriers. The electric field of the stationary charges produces drift\nthat counteracts diffusion. At thermal equilibrium, the net current\nvanishes.<\/p>\n<p>The p terminal and n terminal of a pn diode are called the\n<strong>anode<\/strong> and <strong>cathode<\/strong>, respectively. We\ndefine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_D=u_A-u_K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand count\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas positive from anode to cathode. With a positive external voltage, the\npotential barrier at the junction decreases. The forward current can\nincrease sharply. With a negative voltage, the blocking effect\nincreases. A small reverse current and, at sufficiently high reverse\nvoltage, possible breakdown are part of real diode behaviour.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three computational models<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model<\/th>\n<th>Forward conduction<\/th>\n<th>Reverse blocking<\/th>\n<th>Application<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ideal switch<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_D=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_D\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_D=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_D\\le0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Determine current paths and polarity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constant forward voltage drop<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_D=U_F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_D\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_D=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_D\\le U_F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Estimate within a specified current range<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exponential characteristic<\/td>\n<td>Current increases with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Small negative current before breakdown<\/td>\n<td>Analyse operating points and changes in current<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Within its applicable range, the <strong>Shockley equation<\/strong>\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_D=I_S\\left(\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{u_D}{\\eta U_T}\\right)-1\\right),\n\\qquad U_T=\\frac{k_\\mathrm{B}T}{q}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the saturation current,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe ideality factor,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe absolute temperature and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe thermal voltage. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnear\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>25.85<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_T\\approx25.85\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIndependently of the following rectifier model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_S=10\\,\\mathrm{nA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_D=0.60\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyield a current of approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.10\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA constant voltage drop is thus an approximation for a particular\noperating range rather than an exact material constant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: half-wave rectifier<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal source with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s(t)=3.3\\,\\mathrm V\\sin(\\omega t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsupplies a series circuit consisting of a diode and a load\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1.0\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe anode is connected to the source and the cathode to the upper\nterminal of the load. The lower load terminal returns to the source. Use\nthe model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.65<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_F=0.65\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_009_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Source voltage and load voltage over one period. The output follows the positive part of the source voltage minus 0.65 volts, wherever the result is positive.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: In the half-wave rectifier, the diode conducts only\nduring the sufficiently positive half-cycle; the load peak is 0.65 V\nbelow the source peak here.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s&gt;U_F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe loop equation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_R=u_s-U_F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDuring the blocking phase, no current flows through the load, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_R=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCombining these results gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>R<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_R(t)=\\max\\!\\left(0,u_s(t)-U_F\\right),\\qquad i_R(t)=\\frac{u_R(t)}R.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The peak values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.65<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.65\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.65<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.65\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTo calculate the mean, set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arcsin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=\\arcsin(U_F/U_m)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_m=3.3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe conduction angle range is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha&lt;\\omega t&lt;\\pi-\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIntegration over a full period gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo accent=\"true\">¯<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.746<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\overline{u_R}=\n\\frac{2U_m\\cos\\alpha-U_F(\\pi-2\\alpha)}{2\\pi}\n\\approx0.746\\,\\mathrm V.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The output is pulsating. This model does not yet include an\nenergy-storage element for smoothing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Increase the load resistance to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDetermine the peak current and the mean current using the constant\nforward voltage drop model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An ideal bridge topology uses two conducting diodes during each\nhalf-cycle. With the same source and a load of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndetermine the output peak for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.65<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_F=0.65\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper diode.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the diode reverse-biased at a source voltage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3.3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the half-wave rectifier? State\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The output voltage remains the same in the chosen model. The peak\ncurrent is halved to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.325<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.325\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the mean to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.373<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.373\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The voltage remaining across the load branch is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.65<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.3\\,\\mathrm V-2\\cdot0.65\\,\\mathrm V=2.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With zero load current, the cathode is at the lower reference\npotential. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_D=-3.3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the blocking condition is satisfied.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations and next step<\/h2>\n<p>Forward voltage, reverse current and switching behaviour vary with\ntemperature, current and frequency. A real rectifier can also be\naffected by the source impedance. If the requirements differ, the\ncomponent model must account for these effects. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">The next\nlesson on transistors<\/a> adds a third terminal through which a current\npath can be controlled.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/9-7-semiconductor-devices\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 3, § 9.7 “Semiconductor Devices”<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section\n9.7 – depletion region, bias and diode characteristic. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-071j-introduction-to-electronics-signals-and-measurement-spring-2006/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.071J “Introduction to Electronics, Signals, and\nMeasurement”, lectures 22–23<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · nonlinear components and diode\ncircuits. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M3-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M3-L3 – Transistors as Switches and Amplifiers<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Semiconductors, Analogue Electronics and Digital Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Diode\ncharacteristics and model ranges<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Kirchhoff’s\nvoltage and current laws<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare BJTs and MOSFETs, verify\nthe operating region before calculating, and determine the operating\npoint and small-signal change for an amplifier.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two devices with three terminals<\/h2>\n<p>The terminals of an NPN bipolar junction transistor (BJT) are called\nthe base, collector and emitter. A forward-biased base-emitter junction\ninjects charge carriers. With the base-collector junction\nreverse-biased, the collector collects most of these carriers. In this\nforward-active region,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_C\\approx\\beta I_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a simple model with a current gain of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the collector voltage falls too far, the base-collector junction can\nalso become forward-biased. The BJT then reaches\n<strong>saturation<\/strong>. The relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_C=\\beta I_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nno longer predicts the collector current there.<\/p>\n<p>In an n-channel enhancement-mode MOSFET, an electric field at the\ninsulated gate controls the formation of a channel between drain and\nsource. The simple DC model sets the gate current to zero. During\nswitching, however, the gate capacitance must be charged or discharged.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{GS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbelow the model threshold\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{th}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe nMOS is approximately off. Above the threshold, its behaviour also\ndepends on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{DS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role<\/th>\n<th>BJT<\/th>\n<th>nMOS with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{DS}\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Switch in the off state<\/td>\n<td>No significant base or collector current<\/td>\n<td>Simplified:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{GS}&lt;U_\\mathrm{th}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conducting switch with a small voltage drop<\/td>\n<td>Usually BJT saturation<\/td>\n<td>Small\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{DS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the ohmic region with sufficient gate drive<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analogue amplifier<\/td>\n<td>Forward-active region<\/td>\n<td>Possible operation in MOSFET saturation with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{DS}\\ge u_{GS}-U_\\mathrm{th}&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The same term, “saturation”, denotes different models for the BJT and\nthe MOSFET.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Determining switching states from the external circuit<\/h2>\n<p>An NPN transistor has a grounded emitter, a collector resistor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconnected to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a base resistor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconnected to a control voltage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAssume approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{BE}=0.70\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt follows that<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_B=13\\,\\mathrm{\\mu A},\\qquad\nI_C\\approx1.30\\,\\mathrm{mA},\\qquad\nU_{CE}=5\\,\\mathrm V-I_C(1.0\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega})=3.70\\,\\mathrm V.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_C&gt;U_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconfirms the forward-active assumption. If the base resistance is\nreduced to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the same control voltage, the active-region formula would demand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>13<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">13\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the saturation model, the load network here supplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.8\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{CE}=0.20\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor this heavily driven case, the active-region approach is incompatible\nwith the loop equation.<\/p>\n<p>For an nMOS modelled as a switch with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{on}=100\\,\\mathrm\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto ground and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe on state gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.55<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_D=4.55\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.455<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{out}=I_D R_\\mathrm{on}=0.455\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the off state, the upper resistor pulls the output up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe stated on-resistance applies only with the sufficient gate drive\nassumed by the model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Operating point and small-signal behaviour of the nMOS<\/h2>\n<p>For an nMOS with grounded source and no channel-length modulation, we\nuse the following equations in MOSFET saturation:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_D=\\frac{k}{2}(U_{GS}-U_\\mathrm{th})^2,\\qquad\ng_m=k(U_{GS}-U_\\mathrm{th}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the local slope of the drain current with respect to the gate\nvoltage. Choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=2.0\\,\\mathrm{mA/V^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\mathrm{th}=1.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na gate bias of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na supply of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a drain resistance of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_D=1.0\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{DS}=3.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is greater than the overdrive voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{GS}-U_\\mathrm{th}=1.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe saturation formula can therefore be applied.<\/p>\n<p>The small-signal value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m=2.0\\,\\mathrm{mS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn increase in gate voltage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases the drain current by about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\,\\mathrm{\\mu A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand decreases the output voltage by about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">80\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe exact model yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0404<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0404\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.9192<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.9192\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn this example, the linear approximation differs from the exact output\nby less than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_120_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Output characteristic in the saturation region with an operating point at two volts gate voltage and three volts drain voltage, together with a small-signal tangent.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: At the nMOS operating point of 2 V at the gate and 3\nV at the drain, the tangent shows the local negative slope of the\namplifier characteristic, valid only for small changes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The negative slope arises from the voltage drop across the drain\nresistor. The tangent describes only the local gain; the portion shown\nsatisfies the saturation criterion of the model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the output change for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-10\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the gate using the small-signal model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Set the gate bias to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhy does the previous saturation equation not yield a valid operating\npoint?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a current flow briefly at the MOS gate during switching even\nthough the DC model contains no gate current?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta I_D=-20\\,\\mathrm{\\mu A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U_\\mathrm{out}=+40\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The equation would yield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{DS}=-3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis already contradicts the assumed positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{DS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the saturation criterion\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{DS}\\ge2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA model of the ohmic region is needed for the solution.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Gate and terminal capacitances take up or release charge when the\nvoltage changes.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and next step<\/h2>\n<p>The parameters are simulation values. Component variations, the body\nterminal, temperature, operating current and frequency can influence the\nresult. The gain applies locally around the verified operating point. A\nlarge input swing can lead to other regions of the characteristic. In\nthe <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">operational\namplifier<\/a>, amplifier stages are combined into a circuit block whose\nbehaviour can be adjusted by feedback.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-012-microelectronic-devices-and-circuits-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.012 “Microelectronic Devices and Circuits”, lecture\nnotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · MOSFET characteristics, BJT equivalent models and amplifier\nstages. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-002-circuits-and-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.002 “Circuits and Electronics”, lectures 5, 9 and\n10<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · switch model, large-signal operation and small-signal gain. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M3-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M3-L4 – Operational Amplifiers and Analogue Signal Conditioning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Semiconductors, Analogue Electronics and Digital Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Input\nloading<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">equivalent\nsources<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">small-signal\ngain<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> You calculate the gain of an\noperational amplifier circuit with negative feedback and check input\nloading, output current and output swing limits using a sensor\nexample.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three terminals and a power supply<\/h2>\n<p>An operational amplifier has a non-inverting input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan inverting input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_o<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt also requires a power supply. The simplified linear model uses a\nlarge open-loop gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the difference between the input voltages:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_o=A_0(u_+-u_-).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the idealised model, no current flows into the inputs. The output\nswing limit remains a separate condition. If the calculated output lies\noutside its operating range, the linear equation cannot apply there. In\nreal devices, too, the input range and output swing are two distinct\nlimits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Setting the negative feedback<\/h2>\n<p>For a non-inverting amplifier, the signal is applied to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom the output,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nleads to the inverting input. From there,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nleads to reference ground. Since no input current flows in the model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_-=\\beta u_o<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=R_g/(R_f+R_g)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA positive output change increases\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthereby reducing the amplified input difference. This is negative\nfeedback.<\/p>\n<p>Substitution into the output equation gives, for stable linear\noperation,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><munderover><mo>→<\/mo><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>β<\/mi><mo>≫<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><\/mrow><\/munderover><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>β<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{u_o}{u_+}=\\frac{A_0}{1+A_0\\beta}\n\\ \\xrightarrow[A_0\\beta\\gg1]{}\\ \\frac1\\beta=1+\\frac{R_f}{R_g}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_+\\approx u_-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows in this state from the large gain and negative feedback. Without\nthese conditions, it must not be treated as a general property of the\ninputs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Conditioning a sensor source<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a sensor source is modelled as an ideal voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s=0.40\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin series with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s=20\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe downstream circuit has a load of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L=10\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconnected to ground. When connected directly, it reads only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.133<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.40\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot10/(20+10)\\approx0.133\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe resulting error arises from the voltage divider formed by the source\nresistance and the load.<\/p>\n<p>The sensor source now drives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof a non-inverting amplifier with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_f=20\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_g=10\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0=10^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe load is connected to the output. The ideal input gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_+=0.40\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith a high loop gain,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_o<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis close to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3u_+=1.20\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith finite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe formula gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.19964<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_o\\approx1.19964\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the model, the output drives approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120\\,\\mathrm{\\mu A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthrough the load and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\,\\mathrm{\\mu A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthrough the feedback divider. The power supply provides the additional\nenergy.<\/p>\n<p>The model amplifier can only provide output voltages between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+2.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.90\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe linear calculation would give approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.70\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn fact, the assumed output clips at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+2.0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn this state, the inverting input is at about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.667<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.667\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe numbers therefore also show that the inputs are no longer\napproximately equal.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_121_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Amplifier characteristic with a slope of approximately three, output clipping at plus and minus two volts, and two marked sensor values.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The model amplifier characteristic is approximately\nlinear at first but saturates at ±2 V output; a larger input is\nclipped.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The clipped characteristic is a static model. The first sensor value\nlies within the linear range; the second would require a greater output\nswing than is available.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What linear output voltage results at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s=0.40\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>22<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">22\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nUse the high-loop-gain approximation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At approximately what input does positive clipping begin with the\noriginal resistors?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A protection resistor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis inserted between the output and the load. The feedback remains\nconnected to the output before the protection resistor. What is the load\nvoltage at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_o=1.20\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The gain is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>22<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+22/10=3.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe output voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.28<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.28\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand remains within the limit.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.667<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s=2/3\\,\\mathrm V=0.667\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAbove this, a gain of three demands a larger output swing.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The new divider gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.09<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.20\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot10/11\\approx1.09\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the given model, negative feedback does not compensate for the\nvoltage drop beyond its sensing point.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and next step<\/h2>\n<p>For dynamic signals, gain bandwidth, slew rate and stability must be\nconsidered. With real sensors, input bias current, offset, noise and\nintrinsic impedance are additional factors. The steady-state example\ncircuit is a model whose terminal values and load current must be\nchecked before each use. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Combinational\nlogic<\/a> now switches to the abstraction of two logic values. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">The interface\nlesson<\/a> connects the two perspectives.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-002-circuits-and-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.002 “Circuits and Electronics”, lectures 19–20<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\noperational amplifier model and negative feedback. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-071j-introduction-to-electronics-signals-and-measurement-spring-2006/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.071J “Introduction to Electronics, Signals, and\nMeasurement”, lectures 28–29<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · basic circuits and input and output\nimpedance. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M3-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M3-L5 – Boolean Algebra and Combinational Logic<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Semiconductors, Analogue Electronics and Digital Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> The distinction between <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">conducting\nand cut-off transistor models<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> You formulate a combinational\nfunction as a truth table and Boolean expression, simplify it, and\nimplement it using elementary gates or a multiplexer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The decision depends on the inputs<\/h2>\n<p>Two sensors supply valid logic values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA select bit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines which value appears at the output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter the gate propagation delays have elapsed, the same input\ncombination always produces the same output. This behaviour is called\n<strong>combinational<\/strong>. It has no stored state.<\/p>\n<p>In Boolean expressions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>¬<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lnot A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans NOT and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\land B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans AND. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\lor B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nat least one input must be true. This OR includes the case in which both\ninputs are true. With two inputs, exclusive OR\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⊕<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\oplus B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis true exactly when they differ.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The truth table and its terms<\/h2>\n<p>Each row describes one possible combination. Three bits result in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^3=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrows.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A systematic construction starts with every row that yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe term for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(S,A,B)=(0,1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>¬<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>¬<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\lnot S)\\land A\\land(\\lnot B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause only this assignment makes all three factors true. Form\ncorresponding terms for the remaining rows with output one and combine\nthem using OR.<\/p>\n<p>The first two rows with output one can be combined into\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>¬<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\lnot S)\\land A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause they differ only in the value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLikewise, the other two rows with output one give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\land B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe simplified function is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>¬<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∨<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=((\\lnot S)\\land A)\\lor(S\\land B).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is the equation for a multiplexer with two data lines. As a gate\nnetwork, it consists of an inverter for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntwo AND gates and one OR gate. You can test the mapping directly by\nsubstituting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin turn.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_122_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Gate network consisting of an inverter, two AND gates and an OR gate, with data lines A and B, select S and output Y.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The gate network selects input A for S = 0 and input\nB for S = 1; one inverter, two AND gates and one OR gate implement the\nmultiplexer.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The select bit is inverted in the upper branch and fed unchanged into\nthe lower branch. The two AND results are combined at the OR gate. The\ndrawing shows logical connections, not physical wiring or propagation\ndelays.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Enable and implementation with NAND<\/h2>\n<p>An enable input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshould set the output to zero when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis requires only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=E\\land Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe original selection remains effective. Here, the enable input\nswitches between two logical results. The output remains electrically\ndriven even when it supplies zero.<\/p>\n<p>For an implementation using NAND gates,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">NAND<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{NAND}(X,W)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the negation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\land W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDe Morgan’s laws give the following for the multiplexer:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">NAND<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">NAND<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">NAND<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>Y<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">NAND<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nS_n &amp;= \\operatorname{NAND}(S,S),\\\\\nN_A &amp;= \\operatorname{NAND}(S_n,A),\\\\\nN_B &amp;= \\operatorname{NAND}(S,B),\\\\\nY &amp;= \\operatorname{NAND}(N_A,N_B).\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The two intermediate results are the negated AND terms. Their final\nNAND operation therefore produces the OR of the original AND terms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nevaluate both values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen the enable input is also considered?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which of the expressions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\lor B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⊕<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\oplus B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive one when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=B=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Express “neither input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnor input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis set” in two equivalent ways.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=E\\land S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\lor B=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause at least one input is true.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⊕<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\oplus B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause the inputs are equal.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>¬<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lnot(A\\lor B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexpresses the negated OR operation. The equivalent expression is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>¬<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∧<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>¬<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\lnot A)\\land(\\lnot B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and next step<\/h2>\n<p>A truth table describes stable values. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=B=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe intended value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains one. In a gate implementation, paths with different delays may\nnevertheless produce a brief incorrect pulse if one AND branch switches\noff before the other switches on. An electrically invalid input also has\nno guaranteed value in the table. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">The lesson on\nstorage and clocks<\/a> shows how a digital value is captured and held at\ndefined times.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.004 “Computation Structures”, lectures 2–4<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\ndigital abstraction, gates and synthesis. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://computationstructures.org/notes/combinational_logic/notes.html\">Steve\nWard, “Combinational Logic”, Computation Structures<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · truth tables,\nBoolean terms and multiplexers. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M3-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M3-L6 – Storage, Clocks and Finite-State Machines<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Semiconductors, Analogue Electronics and Digital Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Combinational\nlogic and gate propagation delays<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> You distinguish between latches,\nflip-flops and registers, evaluate a timing diagram at active clock\nedges, and trace the states of a simple finite-state machine.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What the circuit retains<\/h2>\n<p>A storage element with feedback can retain information about an input\nvalue after the input has changed. A level-sensitive D latch follows its\ndata input while the enable is active and holds the last captured value\nwhile the enable is inactive. A positive-edge-triggered D flip-flop\ncaptures the data input at the rising edge of its clock. Its output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthen holds the stored value until the next capture. A register groups\nseveral such storage bits, typically sharing a common clock.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reading a clock sequence<\/h2>\n<p>The example clock has a period of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand its rising edges occur at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>10<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>20<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>30<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>40<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOutput\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstarts at zero. The values of data input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nimmediately before these edges are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,0,1,1,0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter each edge. Stable data can change between two edges without\nbecoming immediately visible at the output. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsampling again yields one, so no visible output transition occurs.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_008_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Timing diagram of a D flip-flop. D has successive values one, zero, one, one and zero at the rising clock edges. Q captures them with a delay of one nanosecond.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: At each rising clock edge, Q captures the preceding\nstable D value with a modelled 1 ns delay; the timing diagram shows the\nresulting sequence.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>In the real timing model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be stable during a setup time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_\\mathrm{setup}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore the edge and a hold time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_\\mathrm{hold}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafterwards. A violation can result in an uncertain sampled value or\nmetastability. The clock-to-output delay and the propagation delay of\nthe connected gates also occupy part of the timing budget.<\/p>\n<p>For a synchronous register-to-register path with negligible\ndifferences in clock arrival times, the setup-time estimate is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\mathrm{clk}\\ge t_{\\mathrm{cq,max}}+t_{\\mathrm{pd,max}}+t_\\mathrm{setup}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe idealised result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\mathrm{clk}\\ge8\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncorresponding to at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>125<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">125\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe hold-time condition is additionally checked using minimum\npropagation delays. The maximum frequency alone does not guarantee that\nit is satisfied.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Two consecutive ones<\/h2>\n<p>A warning signal should appear only when a one has been sampled at\ntwo consecutive clock edges. To achieve this, a finite-state machine\nstores one of three states:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor no consecutive ones,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor one and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor at least two ones. A reset sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA state register holds an encoding of the state. Combinational logic\ncalculates the next state from the currently stored state and input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-23\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-23\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-23\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-30\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State before the edge<\/th>\n<th>Next state when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th>Next state when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Output in the state<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The output depends on the currently stored state. This arrangement is\na <strong>Moore machine<\/strong>. With the input sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,0,1,1,1,0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit passes through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_1,S_0,S_1,S_2,S_2,S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter the edges. The observed output values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,0,0,1,1,0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which states and output values follow after reset for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,1,0,1,1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the timing example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhy does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot change at this time?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does the timing budget limit change if the maximum gate\npropagation delay increases to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">States:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_1,S_2,S_0,S_1,S_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe corresponding output is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,1,0,0,1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlies between the active edges at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>10<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe previously stored value is retained.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2+7+1=10\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis required. This gives at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore any additional safety margins.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and next step<\/h2>\n<p>The state model assumes valid bits at each capture. It includes\nneither analogue input threshold evaluation nor protection against\nunstable signals crossing between different clock domains. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">The next\nlesson<\/a> connects the state machine to a modelled sensor signal and\nchecks the electrical validity of the interface.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.004 “Computation Structures”, lectures 5–7<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nsequential logic, storage, state machines and metastability. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://computationstructures.org/notes/sequential_logic/notes.html\">Steve\nWard, “Sequential Logic”, Computation Structures<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · registers and\ntiming constraints. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M3-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M3-L7 – Digital Circuits and Analogue Interfaces<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Semiconductors, Analogue Electronics and Digital Technology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Sensor\nconditioning and negative feedback<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">logic\noperations<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">clocked state\nmachine<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> You combine an analogue\nthreshold decision with a clocked state machine, check voltage levels\nand recognise when the bit model ceases to be valid.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From measurement to decision<\/h2>\n<p>A simulated monitoring system is intended to report a persistently\nelevated sensor voltage. The conditioning stage provides an analogue\nvoltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA threshold circuit converts it into a logic signal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe state machine developed previously checks whether the signal was one\nat at least two consecutive clock edges. The processing thus has two\ntasks: making a decision based on a continuous measurement and making a\ndecision based on a sequence of bits over time.<\/p>\n<p>With a single threshold around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.50\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsmall amounts of noise can cause the signal to switch repeatedly. A\nthreshold circuit with hysteresis uses two limits:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mo>↑<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\uparrow<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the rising threshold and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mo>↓<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\downarrow<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the falling threshold. For the example, we assume\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mo>↑<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.52<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\uparrow=0.52\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mo>↓<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\downarrow=0.48\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Voltage in the model<\/th>\n<th>Output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mo>↑<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s\\ge U_\\uparrow<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mo>↓<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s\\le U_\\downarrow<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mo>↓<\/mo><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mo>↑<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_\\downarrow&lt;u_s&lt;U_\\uparrow<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Previous state<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>These two <strong>sensor voltage thresholds<\/strong> are located\nbefore the digital interface. The electrical voltage limits by which the\nreceiver recognises the output as zero or one belong to a different part\nof the circuit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Checking electrical validity<\/h2>\n<p>For a simplified digital receiver with a supply of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nassume that it must recognise zero up to and including\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\mathrm{IL,max}}=0.80\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\mathrm{IH,min}}=2.00\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nupwards, it must recognise one. Between these limits, no logic value is\nguaranteed for the input. A measured value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.50\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis therefore an electrical fault condition in this model.<\/p>\n<p>With a suitable load, the threshold driver guarantees a low output up\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\mathrm{OL,max}}=0.20\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a high output from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\mathrm{OH,min}}=3.00\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nupwards. This leaves static noise margins of<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nN_L &amp;= V_{\\mathrm{IL,max}}-V_{\\mathrm{OL,max}}=0.60\\,\\mathrm V,\\\\\nN_H &amp;= V_{\\mathrm{OH,min}}-V_{\\mathrm{IH,min}}=1.00\\,\\mathrm V.\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Both values are positive. This calculation refers to the assumed\noutput and input specifications with a common reference ground. It does\nnot yet evaluate edges, propagation delays or loading outside the\nguaranteed range.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Tracing measurements and states<\/h2>\n<p>The output and state machine start at zero and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively. For the discrete example, all listed values are the stable\nvalues immediately before successive clock edges. In between, we assume\nthere are no additional threshold crossings that would change the state\nof the threshold circuit.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Clock edge<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin V<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th>State after the edge<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Alarm<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.47<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.53<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.51<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.49<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.47<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.54<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.55<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>At edge three, the threshold circuit remains at one despite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.51<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.51\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause the voltage has not yet fallen below the lower limit. In\ncontrast, at edge six it holds the value zero. The same intermediate\nvoltage region can therefore produce different outputs depending on its\nhistory.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_123_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Eight sensor values compared with thresholds of 0.48 and 0.52 volts; below them, the discrete X and alarm values at each clock edge.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Thresholds at 0.48 and 0.52 V create hysteresis in\nsignal X; the separate report row adds a clocked decision\nstage.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The points are individual sample values, not a statement about the\nwaveform between edges. The two rows of bits show how hysteresis and the\ntemporal decision perform different functions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Another driver supplies only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.80\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the worst-case high condition. Determine its high noise margin and\nexplain the result.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Starting from reset, what are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the alarm for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.49<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0.53<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0.47<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s=(0.49;0.53;0.50;0.47)\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is demonstrating valid static levels not sufficient for an\nasynchronously arriving input signal?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.80<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_H=1.80-2.00=-0.20\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe receiver does not guarantee a logic one for this high output.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The threshold circuit produces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,1,1,0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe state machine reports\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,0,1,0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter the edges.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Even a transition between two valid levels can occur too close to a\nclock edge and violate the setup or hold condition. Synchronisation with\ncascaded registers can reduce the risk of metastability and introduces\nadditional delay. Short pulses also require an appropriate transfer\nstrategy.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations and recap<\/h2>\n<p>The model uses fixed switching thresholds and simulated inputs. Real\nthresholds vary with manufacturing process, supply voltage and\ntemperature. A threshold circuit can reduce sensitivity to small signal\nchanges, whereas the state machine evaluates the duration of a logic\nstate. These are two distinct properties which must be checked\nseparately. The <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">study\npathway<\/a> places the next section on electromagnetic fields and waves\nin context.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-071j-introduction-to-electronics-signals-and-measurement-spring-2006/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.071J “Introduction to Electronics, Signals, and\nMeasurement”, lecture 33<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · positive feedback and Schmitt triggers. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.004 “Computation Structures”, lectures 2 and 7<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nvoltage-based logic levels, synchronisation and metastability. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
